The extension of the wire when the ball is in water is  

\[
\Delta L = 0.05\ \text{cm}
\]

Young’s modulus \(Y\) relates stress and strain:

\[
Y = \frac{\text{stress}}{\text{strain}}
   = \frac{F/A}{\Delta L/L}
   = \frac{F L}{A \Delta L}
\]

Solving for the original length \(L\):

\[
L = \frac{Y A \Delta L}{F}
\]

The force \(F\) is the weight of the ball.  
Given volume \(V = 500\ \text{cm}^3\) and density of water \(\rho = 1\ \text{g/cm}^3\),

\[
\text{weight } = \rho V g = 500 \times 980 = 4.9\times10^{5}\ \text{dynes}
\]

Now substitute the numbers:

\[
\begin{aligned}
Y &= 7\times10^{11}\ \text{dynes/cm}^2 \\
A &= 2\times10^{-3}\ \text{cm}^2 \\
\Delta L &= 0.05\ \text{cm} \\
F &= 4.9\times10^{5}\ \text{dynes}
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
L = \frac{(7\times10^{11})(2\times10^{-3})(0.05)}{4.9\times10^{5}}
   = \frac{7\times10^{11}\times1\times10^{-4}}{4.9\times10^{5}}
   = \frac{7\times10^{7}}{4.9\times10^{5}}
   \approx 142.9\ \text{cm}
\]

Thus the length of the wire is closest to **142.9 cm**, which corresponds to choice **B**.

