The Laplace transform of the given function is  

\[
f(t)=\frac{1}{s^{3}+1}.
\]

Its inverse transform is  

\[
f(t)=L^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s^{3}+1}\right\}
      =e^{-t/3}\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}\sin\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}t\right),\qquad t\ge 0.
\]

Hence  

\[
\begin{aligned}
\int_{0}^{\tau} f(t)\,dt
   &=\int_{0}^{\tau} e^{-t/3}\frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}
      \sin\!\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}t\right)dt  \\
   &=1-e^{-(\tau)/3}\Bigl[\cos\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)
      +\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)\Bigr].
\end{aligned}
\]

Multiplying by \(\frac{1}{s}\) and taking the inverse Laplace transform gives  

\[
\begin{aligned}
L\Big\{\int_{0}^{\tau} f(t)\,d\tau\Big\}
   &=\frac{1}{s}\Big[1-e^{-(\tau)/3}
      \Big(\cos\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)
      +\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)\Bigr)\Big] \\
   &\xrightarrow{\;\mathcal{L}^{-1}\;}\;
      \frac{1}{s^{2}}+\frac{1}{s}
        e^{-\tau/3}\Big(\cos\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)
        +\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\sin\!\Bigl(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}\tau\Bigr)\Big).
\end{aligned}
\]

\[
\boxed{\frac{1}{s^{2}}+\frac{1}{s}\,e^{-\frac{\tau}{3}}
\left(\cos\!\frac{\sqrt{3}\,\tau}{3}
+\frac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\sin\!\frac{\sqrt{3}\,\tau}{3}\right)}
\]

